In Java Streams, map() and filter() are two very common operations but they do different things.

| Feature              | `map()`            | `filter()`                         |
| -------------------- | ------------------ | ---------------------------------- |
| Purpose              | Transform data     | Select data                        |
| Output size          | Same as input size | Can be smaller                     |
| Returns              | Modified elements  | Only elements that match condition |
| Functional Interface | `Function<T, R>`   | `Predicate<T>`                     |

1️⃣ map() – Transform Each Element

map() is used when you want to convert or transform each element of a stream.

Example
List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(1,2,3,4);

List<Integer> squares = numbers.stream()
        .map(n -> n * n)
        .collect(Collectors.toList());

System.out.println(squares);
Output
[1, 4, 9, 16]

✔ Each element is transformed.

Flow

Input  : [1,2,3,4]
map    : n -> n*n
Output : [1,4,9,16]
2️⃣ filter() – Select Elements

filter() is used to keep only elements that satisfy a condition.

Example
List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(1,2,3,4,5,6);

List<Integer> even = numbers.stream()
        .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
        .collect(Collectors.toList());

System.out.println(even);
Output
[2,4,6]

✔ Only elements that match condition remain.

Flow

Input  : [1,2,3,4,5,6]
filter : n % 2 == 0
Output : [2,4,6]
3️⃣ Using map() and filter() Together

Very common in real backend code (Spring Boot).

Example

Get square of even numbers

List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(1,2,3,4,5,6);

List<Integer> result = numbers.stream()
        .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
        .map(n -> n * n)
        .collect(Collectors.toList());

System.out.println(result);
Output
[4,16,36]

Flow

Input      : [1,2,3,4,5,6]
filter     : [2,4,6]
map(square): [4,16,36]
4️⃣ Real Backend Example (Very Common Interview)
Suppose we have a User class
class User {
    String name;
    int age;
}
Get names of users older than 18
List<String> names = users.stream()
        .filter(u -> u.getAge() > 18)
        .map(User::getName)
        .collect(Collectors.toList());

Step 1: filter() → select adults
Step 2: map() → extract names

5️⃣ Simple Way to Remember
filter → remove elements
map    → modify elements

Example:

[1,2,3,4,5]

filter (even) → [2,4]
map (*10)     → [20,40]
6️⃣ Interview One-Line Answer

map():

Used to transform each element of a stream.

filter():

Used to select elements from a stream based on a condition.